00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:33.421
Hi, I’m Matt, and I’m not really new here.

00:00:33.421 --> 00:00:36.834
I’ve been a producer for Journey to the
Microcosmos from the very beginning,

00:00:36.834 --> 00:00:39.283
editing videos for the first 
few years of the channel and

00:00:39.283 --> 00:00:41.375
keeping our journey 
running smoothly.

00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:45.122
And some of you maybe have 
even seen me in our livestreams.

00:00:45.122 --> 00:00:48.281
But this is new, right?

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.704
Me, talking to you all?

00:00:50.704 --> 00:00:56.070
I’ll probably be around a bit more along
with some other new voices as our usual voice,

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.732
Hank, takes some time to take care of himself.

00:00:58.732 --> 00:01:03.252
So our journey will sound a bit different,
maybe even look a bit different at times.

00:01:03.252 --> 00:01:08.070
But every journey has to take unfamiliar paths,
and we hope you’ll be able to enjoy this

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.406
one with us.

00:01:09.406 --> 00:01:14.429
And like I said, I spent the first few years
of this channel editing the videos together,

00:01:14.429 --> 00:01:19.170
and in that time, I’ve gotten so much better
at identifying our microbial friends than

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.151
I ever would have imagined.

00:01:22.151 --> 00:01:23.622
Like these organisms.

00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:27.960
Before I worked on this show, if you’d asked
me what they are, I maybe would have told

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.642
you that they look like some long threads.

00:01:30.642 --> 00:01:35.490
But we’ve seen them quite a bit now, watching
them glide across our slides as we talk about

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.174
their sediment homes.

00:01:37.174 --> 00:01:42.727
And that’s how I know that these are a type
of bacteria called Beggiatoa

00:01:47.018 --> 00:01:49.193
There’s a few things that give them away.

00:01:49.193 --> 00:01:53.920
First is the simple serpentine shape of
their bodies, sliding against each other like

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.220
a pile of pythons buried on a microscopy slide.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.370
The second detail that identifies them as
Beggiatoa are those little dots inside of them.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:06.310
They look like bubbles, but they’re actually
sulfur granules.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:11.349
So why do our Beggiatoa friends stuff themselves
full of sulfur?

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:14.810
To understand that, we have to first talk
about where they live.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.640
James, our master of microscopes, usually
finds his Beggiatoa at the bottom of his fish

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.204
tank, where their accumulated bodies turn
the sediment white.

00:02:23.204 --> 00:02:28.477
And in the wild, Beggiatoa can be found in
both freshwater and marine environments,

00:02:28.477 --> 00:02:30.865
layering themselves upon 
each other to form mats

00:02:30.865 --> 00:02:35.173
that can end up looking 
like nature’s weirdest shag rug.

00:02:35.173 --> 00:02:38.790
They gather in the area of water that will
give them the access they need to particular

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.221
nutrients, but…

00:02:40.221 --> 00:02:41.971
what does that mean exactly?

00:02:41.971 --> 00:02:45.627
We all like to live in a spot that gives us
the access we need to particular nutrients,

00:02:45.627 --> 00:02:49.392
but we don’t all form clumps and mats on
the bottom of fish tanks.

00:02:49.392 --> 00:02:55.001
So what is it about their particular location
that particularly suits the Beggiatoa?

00:02:57.104 --> 00:03:03.379
The answer came in the year 1887 from a scientist
named Sergei Winogradsky.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:09.650
Winogradsky is sometimes credited with discovering
Beggiatoa, but from what we’ve read, scientists

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:13.430
had been describing and studying Beggiatoa
before he did.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:17.510
In 1870, one researcher described the fact
that those granules would increase in number

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:20.148
when the Beggiatoa was in its normal habitat.

00:03:20.148 --> 00:03:24.209
There was something about these sulfur granules
that made sense given that these Beggiatoa

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:27.655
were found in sulfur-rich environments, like
sulfur springs.

00:03:27.655 --> 00:03:31.350
And when other scientists began to look at
bacteria in those environments, they

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.398
found similar granules.

00:03:33.398 --> 00:03:38.319
But the creation and purpose of those granules
remained unclear.

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:42.929
Winogradsky did not first encounter Beggiatoa
with the intention of solving those mysteries.

00:03:42.929 --> 00:03:48.600
In 1885, he began working for the botanist
Anton de Bary at the University of Strassburg.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.480
The question that Winogradsky was initially
meant to pursue revolved around the shape

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.826
of bacteria, and really just how fixed that
shape is.

00:03:56.826 --> 00:04:01.400
To answer that question, Winogradsky began
traveling around Switzerland and Germany to

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.278
gather Beggiatoa growing in sulfur springs.

00:04:04.278 --> 00:04:08.670
But then he got a bit distracted and wandered
down a different path.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.569
Winogradsky needed to figure out how to keep
the Beggiatoa he was studying alive in the

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:17.359
lab, so he grew them in tiny little cultures
and watched as the Beggiatoa filaments stuck

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:19.650
to the side of their chambers.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:23.610
He would wash them with various fluids with
different compositions, and as he watched

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:28.204
them, he could begin to see what nutrients
best sustained the Beggiatoa.

00:04:28.204 --> 00:04:33.660
And as he watched, Winogradsky’s curiosity
around the sulfur granules grew as he asked

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.933
more and more questions about how they got
inside the organisms, and what their role is.

00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:43.229
In one of his experiments, Winogradsky kept
different batches of cells in different types

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:46.699
of spring water, and then monitored them under
the microscope.

00:04:46.699 --> 00:04:51.280
He found that the cells grown in the presence
of hydrogen sulfide would end up full of those

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.780
sulfur granules.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:56.527
But cells grown in spring water full of calcium
sulfate didn’t.

00:04:56.527 --> 00:05:00.490
That experiment showed that the Beggiatoa
were getting their sulfur granules by oxidizing

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.226
hydrogen sulfide.

00:05:02.226 --> 00:05:07.540
This also explains how the bacteria find their
ideal spot in the water, a narrow band where

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:13.000
hydrogen sulfide and oxygen are both present,
and that allows Beggiatoa access to both chemicals

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.740
they need to form those sulfur granules.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:20.550
But this still didn’t explain what the Beggiatoa
do with the sulfur granules.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:24.880
Winogradsky could see that when the Beggiatoa
were deprived of hydrogen sulfide, those granules

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.095
would disappear…

00:05:27.095 --> 00:05:30.280
so where did they go?

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.595
Were they released into the environment?

00:05:32.595 --> 00:05:35.806
Were they absorbed by the cell in some way?

00:05:35.806 --> 00:05:38.552
What was the point of the sulfur?

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:49.400
What Winogradsky found is that the sulfur
was essentially food for the Beggiatoa, fueling

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.340
their cellular respiration.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:57.339
This was the first ever description of what
would later be known as chemolithotrophy,

00:05:57.339 --> 00:05:59.463
and it was groundbreaking.

00:05:59.463 --> 00:06:05.692
It meant that Winogradsky had found an organism
that could draw energy from an inorganic compound—

00:06:05.692 --> 00:06:11.568
in this case, using sulfur to drive the reactions
it needs to produce energy.

00:06:11.568 --> 00:06:15.910
Eventually, scientists discovered more organisms
that could use sulfur in this way, as well

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:19.336
as others that relied on different inorganic
compounds like nitrogen.

00:06:19.336 --> 00:06:23.729
And in doing so, they are able to convert
these chemicals into biological forms that

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:27.910
can travel through ecosystems and then back
into the environment.

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:33.240
For sulfur, that journey will take it through
many lives, as part of rocks and amino acids

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:38.886
and even acid rain, transforming our lives
and landscapes in the process.

00:06:38.886 --> 00:06:43.740
Organisms like Beggiatoa usher sulfur through
that odyssey, a mundane act of consumption

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.210
that shapes our entire world.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:51.220
And so perhaps it's fitting to end with Winogradsky’s
own words, a moment from his memoir where

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.900
he describes the moment of discovery.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:07:00.770
The work was humdrum, it dragged on and on
sluggishly, and all of a sudden it developed

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:03.904
into an interesting result and was finished.

00:07:03.904 --> 00:07:09.527
All the beating around suddenly made sense,
and I matured in my own eyes.

00:07:09.527 --> 00:07:15.000
Even so, I could not see that my discovery
would become an epoch-making discovery,

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.520
would determine the course of all my future work,
and that it would open a new chapter in microbiology

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:21.841
and physiology.

